(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["tools",{"c:json-mode-versus-schema-enforced-structured-outp":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>There are three levels of \"give me JSON\". \u003cb>Prompting\u003c/b> for JSON guarantees nothing. \u003cb>JSON mode\u003c/b> guarantees syntactically valid JSON but not your keys or types. \u003cb>Structured outputs\u003c/b> (schema-enforced, or \u003ci>strict\u003c/i>) guarantee the output matches your JSON Schema, because the provider only lets the model generate tokens that keep the output valid. Use structured outputs whenever the provider supports them, and remember that a valid shape is not a correct value.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Three levels compared\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\u003cth>How you ask\u003c/th>\u003cth>What is guaranteed\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical failures\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Prompt only\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Return JSON with keys name and email\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nothing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Code fences, prose before the JSON, renamed keys, wrong types\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>JSON mode\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI: \u003ccode>response_format: {\"type\": \"json_object\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Parses as JSON (barring truncation)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Missing or extra keys, wrong types, invented enum values\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Structured outputs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A JSON Schema with strict mode on\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Matches the schema: keys, types, enums, required fields\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Wrong \u003ci>values\u003c/i>, refusals, truncation at the token limit\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>JSON mode has a quirk worth knowing: OpenAI requires the word \"JSON\" to appear somewhere in your messages, and without such an instruction the model can emit whitespace until it hits the token limit. JSON mode is now mainly a fallback for older or local models.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How constrained decoding works\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At every step a model produces a probability for each token in its vocabulary, and one token is sampled. With structured outputs, the provider first compiles your schema into a \u003cb>grammar\u003c/b>: a state machine that knows, at each position, which characters may come next. Before each token is sampled, every token that would break the grammar has its probability set to zero.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Suppose the schema says \u003ccode>currency\u003c/code> is one of \u003ccode>\"EUR\"\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>\"GBP\"\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>\"USD\"\u003c/code>, and the model has generated \u003ccode>{\"currency\": \"\u003c/code>. Only tokens that begin one of those three strings are allowed. The model cannot write \u003ccode>\"usd\"\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>\"US Dollars\"\u003c/code> or a comment, because those tokens are masked out. Valid output is guaranteed by construction, not by persuasion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Three practical consequences follow:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The first request with a new schema is slower\u003c/b> while the grammar compiles. Providers cache compiled grammars (Anthropic keeps them for 24 hours after last use).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Not all of JSON Schema is supported.\u003c/b> Unsupported features are rejected with a 400 error, not ignored.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The model is forced to fill the shape even when the input does not fit it.\u003c/b> OpenAI's docs warn that an input unrelated to the schema can produce hallucinated values. Give the model a legitimate way out: nullable fields, a \u003ccode>\"not_found\"\u003c/code> enum value, or an \u003ccode>is_invoice: false\u003c/code> flag.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Local servers do the same thing: vLLM, SGLang, llama.cpp and Ollama all support JSON Schema or grammar-constrained generation, using libraries such as XGrammar, Outlines and llguidance under the hood.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A strict extraction request\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An OpenAI Chat Completions request that extracts an invoice into a typed record:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"model\": \"&lt;model&gt;\",\n  \"messages\": [\n    {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Extract the invoice fields. Use null for any field not printed on the invoice.\"},\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"&lt;invoice&gt;ACME Ltd. Invoice INV-2041, issued 2 October 2026. 2 x Widget at $4.50. Total due: USD 9.00&lt;/invoice&gt;\"}\n  ],\n  \"response_format\": {\n    \"type\": \"json_schema\",\n    \"json_schema\": {\n      \"name\": \"invoice\",\n      \"strict\": true,\n      \"schema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"invoice_number\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n          \"issue_date\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"ISO date, YYYY-MM-DD\"},\n          \"currency\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"EUR\", \"GBP\", \"USD\"]},\n          \"total\": {\"type\": \"number\"},\n          \"po_number\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"], \"description\": \"Purchase order number, or null\"}\n        },\n        \"required\": [\"invoice_number\", \"issue_date\", \"currency\", \"total\", \"po_number\"],\n        \"additionalProperties\": false\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The reply arrives as a JSON string in \u003ccode>choices[0].message.content\u003c/code>, for example \u003ccode>{\"invoice_number\":\"INV-2041\",\"issue_date\":\"2026-10-02\",\"currency\":\"USD\",\"total\":9.0,\"po_number\":null}\u003c/code>, with \u003ccode>finish_reason: \"stop\"\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>message.refusal\u003c/code> null. The same idea in other APIs, as of late 2026:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>API\u003c/th>\u003cth>Where the schema goes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI Chat Completions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>response_format: {\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"json_schema\": {\"name\", \"strict\": true, \"schema\"}}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI Responses\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>text: {\"format\": {\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"name\", \"strict\": true, \"schema\"}}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Anthropic Messages\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>output_config: {\"format\": {\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"schema\"}}\u003c/code> (the older beta \u003ccode>output_format\u003c/code> parameter is deprecated)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Gemini\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>responseMimeType: \"application/json\"\u003c/code> plus \u003ccode>responseJsonSchema\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>generateContent\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>response_format\u003c/code> in the newer Interactions API\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Strict-mode schema rules\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Every object needs \u003ccode>\"additionalProperties\": false\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>On OpenAI, \u003cb>every property must be listed in \u003ccode>required\u003c/code>\u003c/b>. Express an optional field as a union with null: \u003ccode>\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]\u003c/code>. Anthropic allows optional properties but outputs required ones first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Size limits differ. OpenAI allows up to 5,000 object properties, 10 levels of nesting and 1,000 enum values per schema. Anthropic limits a request to 20 strict tools, 24 optional parameters and 16 parameters with union types across all its strict schemas.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Constraints differ too. Anthropic does not enforce \u003ccode>minimum\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>maximum\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>minLength\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>maxLength\u003c/code>; its SDK moves them into descriptions, so validate them yourself afterwards (next lesson).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Edge cases you still handle\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Refusals:\u003c/b> on safety grounds the model may refuse. OpenAI puts the text in a separate \u003ccode>refusal\u003c/code> field; Anthropic returns \u003ccode>stop_reason: \"refusal\"\u003c/code>, and the output may not match the schema.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Truncation:\u003c/b> if the output limit is hit, the JSON is incomplete (\u003ccode>finish_reason: \"length\"\u003c/code> on Chat Completions, \u003ccode>stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"\u003c/code> on Anthropic). Raise the limit and retry.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Enum casing:\u003c/b> Anthropic notes it does not guarantee the capitalisation of enum values; compare case-insensitively.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Wrong values:\u003c/b> a perfectly shaped record can still have the wrong total. Validate business rules in code.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you extract a typed record with a strict schema?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Write a JSON Schema for the record with every property typed, closed sets as enums, optional values as unions with null, every property listed in \u003ccode>required\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>additionalProperties: false\u003c/code>. Send it as a \u003ccode>json_schema\u003c/code> response format with \u003ccode>strict: true\u003c/code> (OpenAI) or in \u003ccode>output_config.format\u003c/code> (Anthropic), with instructions saying what to do when a field is missing. Parse the returned JSON, check for a refusal and for truncation, then validate business rules such as totals before using the record.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does JSON mode guarantee, and what does it not?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It guarantees the output is syntactically valid JSON (unless truncated). It does not guarantee your keys, types, required fields or enum values, so you still need a schema validator and retries, or better, schema-enforced structured outputs.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can a strict schema cause a hallucinated value, and how do you prevent it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Constrained decoding forces the model to produce every required field in the right type, even when the input does not contain that information, so it may invent plausible values. Provide a legitimate escape: nullable fields, a \"not found\" enum value or a flag saying the document is not of the expected kind, and tell the model when to use it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:pydantic-models-as-output-contracts":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>Pydantic\u003c/b> model is a Python class that declares the shape of your data with type hints. It is the single source of truth for an LLM output: it generates the JSON Schema you send to the provider, it validates whatever comes back, and it hands your code a typed object. Field descriptions become part of the prompt, and validators enforce the business rules a schema cannot express.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>One model, three jobs\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from datetime import date\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict, Field, model_validator\n\nclass LineItem(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")\n    description: str\n    quantity: int = Field(gt=0)\n    unit_price: float = Field(ge=0, description=\"Price per unit in the invoice currency\")\n\nclass Invoice(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")\n    invoice_number: str = Field(description=\"As printed, e.g. INV-2041\")\n    issue_date: date\n    currency: Literal[\"EUR\", \"GBP\", \"USD\"]\n    vendor_name: str\n    po_number: str | None = Field(description=\"Purchase order number, or null if none is printed\")\n    line_items: list[LineItem]\n    total: float\n\n    @model_validator(mode=\"after\")\n    def total_matches_items(self):\n        expected = round(sum(i.quantity * i.unit_price for i in self.line_items), 2)\n        if abs(expected - self.total) &gt; 0.01:\n            raise ValueError(f\"total {self.total} does not equal the sum of line items {expected}\")\n        return self\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Job 1, the schema.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>Invoice.model_json_schema()\u003c/code> produces the JSON Schema the provider needs. An excerpt of the real output:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"$defs\": {\"LineItem\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"...\": \"...\"}, \"type\": \"object\"}},\n  \"additionalProperties\": false,\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"invoice_number\": {\"description\": \"As printed, e.g. INV-2041\", \"title\": \"Invoice Number\", \"type\": \"string\"},\n    \"issue_date\": {\"format\": \"date\", \"title\": \"Issue Date\", \"type\": \"string\"},\n    \"currency\": {\"enum\": [\"EUR\", \"GBP\", \"USD\"], \"title\": \"Currency\", \"type\": \"string\"},\n    \"po_number\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}],\n                  \"description\": \"Purchase order number, or null if none is printed\", \"title\": \"Po Number\"},\n    \"line_items\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/LineItem\"}, \"title\": \"Line Items\", \"type\": \"array\"}\n  },\n  \"required\": [\"invoice_number\", \"issue_date\", \"currency\", \"vendor_name\", \"po_number\", \"line_items\", \"total\"],\n  \"type\": \"object\"\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>extra=\"forbid\"\u003c/code> became \u003ccode>additionalProperties: false\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>Literal\u003c/code> became an enum; the nested model became a \u003ccode>$ref\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>str | None\u003c/code> without a default became a required but nullable field, exactly what OpenAI's strict mode wants. The descriptions travel with the schema, so the model reads them: they are prompt text.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Job 2, validation.\u003c/b> Feed the model's reply to \u003ccode>Invoice.model_validate_json(text)\u003c/code>. Here is real output for a reply with three mistakes:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>3 validation errors for Invoice\nissue_date\n  Input should be a valid date or datetime, month value is outside expected range of 1-12\n  [type=date_from_datetime_parsing, input_value='2026-13-02', input_type=str]\ncurrency\n  Input should be 'EUR', 'GBP' or 'USD' [type=literal_error, input_value='usd', input_type=str]\nline_items.0.quantity\n  Input should be greater than 0 [type=greater_than, input_value=0, input_type=int]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>And for a reply whose fields are all well-formed but whose total is wrong, the model validator fires: \u003ccode>Value error, total 10.0 does not equal the sum of line items 9.0\u003c/code>. Each error names the exact location (\u003ccode>line_items.0.quantity\u003c/code>), which is precisely what you send back to the model when you ask it to fix the output (next lesson).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Job 3, typed objects.\u003c/b> On success you get an \u003ccode>Invoice\u003c/code> whose \u003ccode>issue_date\u003c/code> is a real \u003ccode>datetime.date\u003c/code> and whose \u003ccode>line_items[0].quantity\u003c/code> is an \u003ccode>int\u003c/code>. The rest of your code never touches raw JSON.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>SDK helpers do the plumbing\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You rarely send \u003ccode>model_json_schema()\u003c/code> by hand:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Library\u003c/th>\u003cth>Call\u003c/th>\u003cth>Where the parsed object is\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI SDK, Chat Completions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>client.chat.completions.parse(model=..., messages=..., response_format=Invoice)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>completion.choices[0].message.parsed\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>.refusal\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI SDK, Responses\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>client.responses.parse(model=..., input=..., text_format=Invoice)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>response.output_parsed\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Anthropic SDK\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>client.messages.parse(model=..., max_tokens=..., messages=..., output_format=Invoice)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>response.parsed_output\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Instructor (many providers)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>instructor.from_provider(\"openai/&lt;model&gt;\").create(response_model=Invoice, max_retries=2, messages=...)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The returned \u003ccode>Invoice\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pydantic AI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Agent(\"openai:&lt;model&gt;\", output_type=Invoice).run_sync(prompt)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>result.output\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The SDKs convert the model into the provider's dialect. Anthropic's SDK, for example, moves constraints it cannot enforce (such as \u003ccode>gt=0\u003c/code>) into descriptions and then validates the response locally, so the constraint is still checked. OpenAI's \u003ccode>parse()\u003c/code> raises an error if the response was cut off by the length limit or a content filter.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Designing good contracts\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Name fields for the model,\u003c/b> not your database: \u003ccode>issue_date\u003c/code> beats \u003ccode>dt1\u003c/code>. Add descriptions with formats and examples.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Use \u003ccode>Literal\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Enum\u003c/code> for closed sets\u003c/b> so invalid categories are impossible.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Make absence explicit\u003c/b> with nullable fields, rather than relying on omitted keys.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Order matters.\u003c/b> Outputs follow schema order (Anthropic puts required fields first). Put an \u003ccode>evidence\u003c/code> or short \u003ccode>explanation\u003c/code> field \u003ci>before\u003c/i> the answer field if you want the model to ground itself first, but keep it short: Anthropic warns that a field asking for full step-by-step reasoning can trigger a refusal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keep nesting shallow.\u003c/b> Deep optional structures are harder for the model and, on Anthropic, count against grammar complexity limits.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Validate business rules in validators,\u003c/b> such as totals and date ranges, because the schema only checks shape.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Define a nested model and validate a model response against it. What does that look like, and what do you get back?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Define an \u003ccode>Invoice\u003c/code> model with typed fields, a \u003ccode>Literal\u003c/code> currency and a \u003ccode>line_items: list[LineItem]\u003c/code> field, where \u003ccode>LineItem\u003c/code> is its own model, with \u003ccode>extra=\"forbid\"\u003c/code> on both and a model validator checking that the total equals the sum of the items. Call \u003ccode>Invoice.model_validate_json(reply_text)\u003c/code>: on success you get a typed \u003ccode>Invoice\u003c/code> object; on failure a \u003ccode>ValidationError\u003c/code> listing each problem with its location (for example \u003ccode>line_items.0.quantity: Input should be greater than 0\u003c/code>), which you can log or send back to the model.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do field descriptions in a Pydantic model affect output quality?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>They are copied into the JSON Schema that is sent to the model, so the model reads them as instructions about each field's meaning and format. A clear description is prompt engineering at the field level.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you express \"optional\" for OpenAI's strict mode in Pydantic?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Declare the field as \u003ccode>str | None\u003c/code> without a default (optionally with a \u003ccode>Field(description=...)\u003c/code>). Pydantic emits it as required but nullable (an \u003ccode>anyOf\u003c/code> with null), which satisfies strict mode's rule that every property is required while letting the model return null.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:validation-repair-and-retries":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Even with structured outputs, some responses will be unusable: broken JSON from a model without strict mode, a total that does not add up, a refusal, a reply cut off at the token limit. Handle them with a ladder: \u003cb>parse and validate\u003c/b>, \u003cb>repair syntax locally\u003c/b>, \u003cb>re-ask with the exact validation errors\u003c/b> (at most two or three times), then \u003cb>fall back\u003c/b> to a stronger model or a human review queue. Never pass unvalidated output downstream, and never retry forever.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why you still validate\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Schema enforcement removes most format errors, but not all of the reasons output can be wrong:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Many models and local servers are used with JSON mode or plain prompting, which do not enforce a schema.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Business rules (totals, date ranges, an id that must exist in your database) cannot be expressed in JSON Schema.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Constraints may be dropped in translation; Anthropic's strict mode, for example, does not enforce \u003ccode>minimum\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>maxLength\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Refusals and truncation produce output that may not match the schema at all.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>So the rule is simple: every response goes through your Pydantic model before your code touches it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The repair ladder\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model reply\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Repair syntax locally\u003c/b>\u003csmall>json-repair\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Validate\u003c/b>\u003csmall>Pydantic model and validators\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Use the object\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Validation failed\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Re-ask\u003c/b>\u003csmall>append the errors, at most 2 times\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Still failing\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Fallback\u003c/b>\u003csmall>stronger model or review queue\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>What went wrong\u003c/th>\u003cth>How you notice\u003c/th>\u003cth>What to do\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Broken syntax (trailing comma, code fence, unclosed brace)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>JSON parse error\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Repair locally with a library such as \u003ccode>json-repair\u003c/code>; no model call needed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Wrong shape or type\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pydantic \u003ccode>ValidationError\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Re-ask with the list of errors\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Business rule broken\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your validator raises\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Re-ask with the rule; if it persists, flag for review\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Refusal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>refusal\u003c/code> field or \u003ccode>stop_reason: \"refusal\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Do not repeat the same request; log it and return a safe error or route it elsewhere\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Truncated output\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>finish_reason: \"length\"\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Raise the output limit or split the input; an unchanged retry fails the same way\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Timeout, 429, 5xx\u003c/td>\u003ctd>HTTP error\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retry the transport with backoff (Working with model APIs module); this is not a re-ask\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Local repair is for \u003ci>syntax only\u003c/i>. Never let code silently fill in meaning, such as defaulting a missing currency to USD: that turns a visible error into an invisible wrong answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Re-asking with the error\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The model is good at fixing its own output when told exactly what is wrong. Pydantic's errors name the field and the problem, so send them back:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nfrom json_repair import repair_json        # pip install json-repair\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nfrom pydantic import ValidationError\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef format_errors(e: ValidationError) -&gt; str:\n    return \"\\n\".join(f\"- {'.'.join(map(str, err['loc'])) or '(root)'}: {err['msg']}\"\n                     for err in e.errors())\n\ndef extract(model_cls, document, max_retries=2):\n    messages = [\n        {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Extract the fields as JSON matching this schema:\\n\"\n                                      + json.dumps(model_cls.model_json_schema())},\n        {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"&lt;document&gt;{document}&lt;/document&gt;\"},\n    ]\n    for attempt in range(max_retries + 1):\n        r = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", messages=messages,\n                                           response_format={\"type\": \"json_object\"})\n        choice = r.choices[0]\n        if choice.finish_reason == \"length\":\n            raise RuntimeError(\"output truncated: raise the token limit or split the document\")\n        text = choice.message.content\n        try:\n            return model_cls.model_validate_json(repair_json(text))\n        except ValidationError as e:\n            messages += [\n                {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": text},\n                {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Your JSON failed validation:\\n\" + format_errors(e)\n                                            + \"\\nReturn the corrected JSON object only.\"},\n            ]\n    raise ValueError(f\"still invalid after {max_retries} retries\")   # caller routes to review\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With the \u003ccode>Invoice\u003c/code> model from the previous lesson, a bad first reply produces this re-ask message:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Your JSON failed validation:\n- issue_date: Input should be a valid date or datetime, month value is outside expected range of 1-12\n- currency: Input should be 'EUR', 'GBP' or 'USD'\n- line_items.0.quantity: Input should be greater than 0\nReturn the corrected JSON object only.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The model sees its own previous answer and a precise list of problems, which usually fixes it in one more call. Libraries package exactly this loop. With Instructor it is one argument:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import instructor\n\nclient = instructor.from_provider(\"openai/&lt;model&gt;\")\ninvoice = client.create(response_model=Invoice, max_retries=2,\n                        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"&lt;document&gt;{text}&lt;/document&gt;\"}])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What retries cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>If a fraction q of first attempts fail, and retries fail at about the same rate, the expected number of calls with up to two retries is about 1 + q + q².\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>q = 0.03:  1 + 0.03 + 0.0009 = 1.03 calls per document (about 3% extra)\nq = 0.30:  1 + 0.30 + 0.09   = 1.39 calls per document (about 39% extra)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Re-ask calls are also larger than the first call, because they carry the bad output and the error list. And a retry roughly doubles latency for every user who hits it. A 3% failure rate is fine to absorb; a 30% rate means the prompt, schema or model needs fixing, not more retries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Track four numbers per extraction type: \u003cb>first-pass validity\u003c/b>, \u003cb>repair rate\u003c/b>, \u003cb>retry success rate\u003c/b> and \u003cb>final failure rate\u003c/b>. Break failures down by field: if \u003ccode>issue_date\u003c/code> fails most often, improve its description.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Cap retries (two is typical) and set an overall time budget, so one bad document cannot hold a request for a minute.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Make the final fallback explicit: return a \u003ccode>needs_review\u003c/code> status, put the document in a queue, or try a stronger model once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Log the raw invalid outputs. They are the best test cases for your regression suite.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you handle an invalid response by re-asking with the validation error?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Validate the reply with the Pydantic model. If it raises a \u003ccode>ValidationError\u003c/code>, append the model's invalid reply as an assistant message and a user message listing each error with its field path (for example \u003ccode>line_items.0.quantity: Input should be greater than 0\u003c/code>) plus \"return the corrected JSON only\", then call the model again. Repeat at most two or three times, and if it still fails, stop and route the item to a fallback such as a stronger model or human review. Libraries such as Instructor implement this loop with a \u003ccode>max_retries\u003c/code> argument.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which failures should you not handle by re-asking the same model with the same request?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Refusals (repeating rarely helps; log and route instead), truncation at the token limit (raise the limit or split the input), and transport errors such as timeouts, 429 and 5xx (retry the HTTP call with backoff instead).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">First attempts fail validation 10% of the time and retries fail at the same rate. With up to two retries, how many calls do you expect per document?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 1 + 0.1 + 0.01 = 1.11 calls, so roughly 11% more calls, and somewhat more than 11% more tokens because re-ask calls are longer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:the-tool-calling-loop":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A model cannot run code. With \u003cb>tool calling\u003c/b> (also called function calling) it can ask \u003ci>you\u003c/i> to run it: you describe your functions, the model replies with a structured request naming a function and its arguments, your code runs the function and sends the result back, and the model continues until it answers in plain text. That request, execute, return, repeat cycle is the \u003cb>tool loop\u003c/b>, and every agent is built on it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The cycle\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Request\u003c/b>\u003csmall>messages plus tool definitions\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model\u003c/b>\u003csmall>returns tool calls\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Your code\u003c/b>\u003csmall>validate, check permission, run\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Append results\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Request again\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model\u003c/b>\u003csmall>no more tool calls\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Final answer to the user\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The API is stateless, so each request carries the whole conversation so far, including earlier tool calls and their results, plus the tool definitions every time. Tool definitions are input tokens on every call.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The real message sequence\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Here is a complete round trip in the OpenAI Chat Completions format, which most providers and local servers accept. A customer asks about an order.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Request 1:\u003c/b> system and user messages plus one tool.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"model\": \"&lt;model&gt;\",\n  \"messages\": [\n    {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are Northwind's support assistant. Use tools to look up orders.\"},\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Where is my order 8812?\"}\n  ],\n  \"tools\": [{\n    \"type\": \"function\",\n    \"function\": {\n      \"name\": \"get_order_status\",\n      \"description\": \"Look up the shipping status of one order by its order number. Returns status, carrier and estimated delivery date.\",\n      \"parameters\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"Digits only, e.g. 8812\"}},\n        \"required\": [\"order_id\"],\n        \"additionalProperties\": false\n      },\n      \"strict\": true\n    }\n  }]\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Response 1:\u003c/b> no text, one tool call. The \u003ccode>finish_reason\u003c/code> tells you the model is waiting for you. (Abridged; ids are illustrative.)\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"choices\": [{\n    \"message\": {\n      \"role\": \"assistant\",\n      \"content\": null,\n      \"tool_calls\": [{\n        \"id\": \"call_7Qx2\",\n        \"type\": \"function\",\n        \"function\": {\"name\": \"get_order_status\", \"arguments\": \"{\\\"order_id\\\":\\\"8812\\\"}\"}\n      }]\n    },\n    \"finish_reason\": \"tool_calls\"\n  }]\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Note that \u003ccode>arguments\u003c/code> is a \u003cb>string\u003c/b> containing JSON, not an object. Your code parses it, validates it and runs the function.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Request 2:\u003c/b> the same conversation plus two new messages, the assistant's tool call exactly as received and a \u003ccode>tool\u003c/code> message carrying the result, linked by \u003ccode>tool_call_id\u003c/code>. The \u003ccode>tools\u003c/code> array is sent again.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\"messages\": [\n  {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are Northwind's support assistant. Use tools to look up orders.\"},\n  {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Where is my order 8812?\"},\n  {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": null, \"tool_calls\": [{\n    \"id\": \"call_7Qx2\", \"type\": \"function\",\n    \"function\": {\"name\": \"get_order_status\", \"arguments\": \"{\\\"order_id\\\":\\\"8812\\\"}\"}}]},\n  {\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": \"call_7Qx2\",\n   \"content\": \"{\\\"status\\\": \\\"in_transit\\\", \\\"carrier\\\": \\\"DHL\\\", \\\"eta\\\": \\\"2026-10-30\\\"}\"}\n]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Response 2:\u003c/b> a normal answer, \u003ccode>finish_reason: \"stop\"\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\"choices\": [{\"message\": {\"role\": \"assistant\",\n  \"content\": \"Your order 8812 is on its way with DHL and should arrive on 30 October.\"},\n  \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"}]}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The same round trip on Anthropic\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Anthropic's Messages API has no \u003ccode>tool\u003c/code> role. The tool call is a \u003ccode>tool_use\u003c/code> content block, the turn ends with \u003ccode>stop_reason: \"tool_use\"\u003c/code>, and the result goes back as a \u003ccode>tool_result\u003c/code> block inside the next \u003cb>user\u003c/b> message. Arguments arrive as an object in \u003ccode>input\u003c/code>, not a string. The tool definition uses \u003ccode>input_schema\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>parameters\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\"messages\": [\n  {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Where is my order 8812?\"},\n  {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": [\n    {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"I'll look that up.\"},\n    {\"type\": \"tool_use\", \"id\": \"toolu_01D7\", \"name\": \"get_order_status\", \"input\": {\"order_id\": \"8812\"}}\n  ]},\n  {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [\n    {\"type\": \"tool_result\", \"tool_use_id\": \"toolu_01D7\",\n     \"content\": \"{\\\"status\\\": \\\"in_transit\\\", \\\"carrier\\\": \\\"DHL\\\", \\\"eta\\\": \\\"2026-10-30\\\"}\"}\n  ]}\n]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>OpenAI's Responses API uses a third shape: the output contains a \u003ccode>function_call\u003c/code> item with a \u003ccode>call_id\u003c/code>, and you send back a \u003ccode>function_call_output\u003c/code> item with the same \u003ccode>call_id\u003c/code>. The idea is identical; the Function calling across providers lesson compares all of them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The loop in code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef get_order_status(order_id: str) -&gt; dict:\n    # call your real order system here\n    return {\"order_id\": order_id, \"status\": \"in_transit\", \"carrier\": \"DHL\", \"eta\": \"2026-10-30\"}\n\nTOOLS = {\"get_order_status\": get_order_status}\nTOOL_SPECS = [...]   # the tool definitions shown in request 1\n\ndef run(messages, max_steps=8):\n    for _ in range(max_steps):\n        r = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", messages=messages, tools=TOOL_SPECS)\n        msg = r.choices[0].message\n        if not msg.tool_calls:                                   # final answer\n            messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": msg.content})\n            return msg.content\n        messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": msg.content,\n                         \"tool_calls\": [c.model_dump() for c in msg.tool_calls]})\n        for call in msg.tool_calls:\n            try:\n                fn = TOOLS[call.function.name]\n                result = fn(**json.loads(call.function.arguments))\n                content = json.dumps(result)\n            except Exception as e:                               # errors go back to the model\n                content = json.dumps({\"error\": f\"{type(e).__name__}: {e}\"})\n            messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call.id, \"content\": content})\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"no final answer after {max_steps} steps\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In production, add two things between parsing and calling: validate the arguments against a Pydantic model for that tool, and check that this user is allowed to perform this action (and ask for confirmation before anything irreversible). The model chooses \u003ci>what\u003c/i> to call; your code decides \u003ci>whether\u003c/i> it runs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Rules of the loop\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Append the assistant message with its tool calls before the results,\u003c/b> unchanged. The API rejects a tool result that does not follow a matching call.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Answer every call.\u003c/b> Each \u003ccode>tool_call_id\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>tool_use_id\u003c/code>) needs exactly one result. On Anthropic, results go first in the next user message, before any text.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Parse and validate arguments;\u003c/b> never \u003ccode>eval\u003c/code> them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Return errors as results,\u003c/b> not exceptions. \"Order 1234 not found; order numbers have four digits\" lets the model recover or ask the user.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Bound the loop\u003c/b> with a step limit, and in agents also with token, cost and time budgets (the Loop engineering module goes deeper).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Expect several calls at once.\u003c/b> A single response can contain more than one tool call (next lessons).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you implement call, execute, return result and final answer?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Send the messages with the tool definitions. If the response contains tool calls, append that assistant message unchanged, then for each call parse the JSON arguments, validate them, check permissions, run the matching function and append a tool result tied to the call's id (catching errors and returning them as results). Call the model again with the extended messages and repeat until a response has no tool calls, which is the final answer, with a maximum number of steps so the loop always ends.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What goes wrong if you send the tool result without first appending the assistant message that contained the tool call?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The API rejects the request, because a tool result must respond to a matching tool call in the preceding assistant message. Even where it is not rejected, the model would see a result it never asked for, with no record of the call that produced it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why return tool errors to the model instead of raising them?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The model can often recover: retry with corrected arguments, try another tool or explain the problem to the user. Raising would end the conversation, and a clear error message is information the model can act on.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:designing-tools-for-models":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A tool definition is a prompt. The model knows only what the name, description and parameter schema tell it, and it sees only what the tool returns. Design tools for the model as you would design an API for a new colleague: \u003cb>few tools that match real tasks\u003c/b>, \u003cb>unambiguous names and parameters\u003c/b>, \u003cb>descriptions that say when and how to use them\u003c/b>, \u003cb>compact, high-signal results\u003c/b> and \u003cb>errors that say what to do next\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Tools are not your REST API\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The common mistake is to wrap every backend endpoint as a tool. Endpoints were designed for programs that already know what to do; a model has to plan with them, and every extra call costs a round trip and tokens. Anthropic's examples of better design:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Instead of\u003c/th>\u003cth>Build\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>list_users\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>list_events\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>create_event\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>schedule_event\u003c/code>, which finds availability and books\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One call does the whole workflow\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>read_logs\u003c/code> (returns the whole file)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>search_logs\u003c/code>, which returns matching lines with context\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only relevant text enters the context\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>get_customer_by_id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>list_transactions\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>list_notes\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>get_customer_context\u003c/code>, which compiles recent, relevant information\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model gets what it needs in one call\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each tool should have one clear, distinct purpose. If two tools could plausibly answer the same request, the model will sometimes pick the wrong one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Merging three overlapping tools\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Before: three thin tools with inconsistent parameters (\u003ccode>id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>customer\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cust_id\u003c/code>) and one-line descriptions.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>[\n  {\"name\": \"get_customer_by_id\", \"description\": \"Gets a customer.\",\n   \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}}},\n  {\"name\": \"list_transactions\", \"description\": \"Lists transactions.\",\n   \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"customer\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"limit\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}}}},\n  {\"name\": \"list_notes\", \"description\": \"Lists notes.\",\n   \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"cust_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}}}\n]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>After: one tool, namespaced by system, with a description that says what it returns, when to use it, how to identify the customer and what happens when nothing matches.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"type\": \"function\",\n  \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"crm_get_customer_context\",\n    \"description\": \"Get what you need to help one customer in a single call: profile and plan, recent transactions and support notes. Use it whenever the user asks about a specific customer's account, billing history or past contacts. Identify the customer by customer_id if known, otherwise by email. Results contain the most recent items only; raise max_items for more. Returns an error if no customer matches.\",\n    \"parameters\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"customer_id\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"], \"description\": \"Internal id such as CUS-10422, or null if unknown\"},\n        \"email\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"], \"description\": \"Customer email; used only when customer_id is null\"},\n        \"include\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"profile\", \"transactions\", \"notes\"]},\n                    \"description\": \"Sections to return; request all three unless the question needs fewer\"},\n        \"max_items\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"description\": \"Maximum transactions and notes to return; use 10 unless more are needed, never above 50\"}\n      },\n      \"required\": [\"customer_id\", \"email\", \"include\", \"max_items\"],\n      \"additionalProperties\": false\n    },\n    \"strict\": true\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>One call replaces three, the parameter names are consistent, \u003ccode>include\u003c/code> is an enum so invalid sections are impossible, and the output size is bounded.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Descriptions, names and parameters\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Descriptions are the biggest lever.\u003c/b> Anthropic calls them by far the most important factor in tool performance and suggests at least three or four sentences: what the tool does, when to use it and when not to, what each parameter means and what the tool does \u003ci>not\u003c/i> return. OpenAI's version is the \u003cb>intern test\u003c/b>: could a new intern use the function correctly from the definition alone? If not, add what they would have asked.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unambiguous parameter names:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>user_id\u003c/code> rather than \u003ccode>user\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>order_number\u003c/code> rather than \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Enums and structure prevent invalid states.\u003c/b> OpenAI's example: \u003ccode>toggle_light(on: bool, off: bool)\u003c/code> allows nonsense; one \u003ccode>state\u003c/code> enum does not.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Do not ask the model for values you already know.\u003c/b> If the user is logged in, your code injects their account id when it executes the call; the model never sees or chooses it. That is also a security control.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Namespace by service\u003c/b> when tools span systems: \u003ccode>jira_search\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>asana_search\u003c/code> instead of two tools called \u003ccode>search\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keep the set small.\u003c/b> OpenAI suggests aiming for fewer than 20 functions available at the start of a turn (a soft guideline), and Anthropic recommends consolidating related operations, for example one tool with an \u003ccode>action\u003c/code> parameter instead of \u003ccode>create_pr\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>review_pr\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>merge_pr\u003c/code>. For larger tool libraries, use tool search (the last lesson in this module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Results the model can use\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every byte a tool returns becomes input tokens, on this call and every later call in the conversation.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Return what the next step needs,\u003c/b> with human-readable fields and stable identifiers (a slug or an order number) rather than opaque internal references.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Bound the size\u003c/b> with pagination, filters and truncation with sensible defaults. Claude Code, for example, caps tool responses at 25,000 tokens by default.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Offer a verbosity switch\u003c/b> when callers need different detail. Anthropic's Slack example returns about 206 tokens in \u003ccode>detailed\u003c/code> mode (with the ids needed for follow-up calls) and about 72 in \u003ccode>concise\u003c/code> mode, roughly a third.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Say when you truncated,\u003c/b> and how to get more: \u003ccode>{\"results\": [...], \"truncated\": true, \"note\": \"Showing 20 of 342 matches; add a date range to narrow the search.\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Errors that guide\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An error is the tool's chance to teach the model. Compare:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Unhelpful: {\"error\": \"400 Bad Request\"}\nHelpful:   {\"error\": \"No customer found with email priya@exmple.com. Check the spelling of the\n            domain or look the customer up by customer_id.\"}\nHelpful:   {\"error\": \"Rate limit exceeded. Retry after 60 seconds.\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The helpful versions say what went wrong and what to try next, so the model can fix its call instead of guessing or apologising to the user.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evaluate tools like prompts.\u003c/b> Write realistic tasks, run the model with your tools, read the transcripts and count wrong tool choices, invalid arguments and wasted calls. Anthropic reports that precise refinements to tool descriptions helped Claude 3.5 Sonnet reach a state-of-the-art SWE-bench Verified score at the time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Enforce safety in the tool, not the description.\u003c/b> Validate arguments, check the user's permissions, require confirmation for irreversible actions, and use idempotency keys on writes so a retried call cannot charge a card twice.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Version tool definitions with your prompts, because changing them changes behaviour (and the prompt-cache prefix).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you merge three overlapping tools into one well-described tool?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Identify the workflow they serve together, for example \u003ccode>get_customer_by_id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>list_transactions\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>list_notes\u003c/code> all serving \"help this customer\". Replace them with one namespaced tool such as \u003ccode>crm_get_customer_context\u003c/code> with consistent, unambiguous parameters (customer id or email), an \u003ccode>include\u003c/code> enum for the sections wanted and a \u003ccode>max_items\u003c/code> bound. Write a description of several sentences saying what it returns, when to use it, how to identify the customer and what happens when nothing matches, and have it return compact results with a truncation note and helpful errors.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What makes a tool error message useful to a model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It says specifically what went wrong and what to do next, such as a corrected format, an alternative tool or when to retry, in plain language. Generic codes or stack traces leave the model guessing.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">The user is logged in. Why should \u003ccode>account_id\u003c/code> not be a tool parameter?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Your code already knows it, so asking the model to fill it adds a chance of error and opens a security hole: a prompt injection could make the model act on another account. Inject the value server-side when executing the call.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:parallel-tool-calls-and-tool-choice":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two settings control how a model uses tools. \u003cb>Tool choice\u003c/b> says whether it may, must or must not call tools, or must call one particular tool. \u003cb>Parallel tool calls\u003c/b> let it request several independent calls in one turn, which your code runs concurrently and answers together. Use them deliberately: forcing a tool or allowing parallel side effects at the wrong moment causes bugs that look like model mistakes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Parallel tool calls\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Ask \"Is it raining in Paris and in Bogotá?\" and a capable model returns \u003cb>two\u003c/b> tool calls in one response. Your loop must run both and return both results before calling the model again. On Chat Completions that is one \u003ccode>tool\u003c/code> message per call id; on Anthropic, all \u003ccode>tool_result\u003c/code> blocks go in a \u003cb>single\u003c/b> user message, before any text.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Running them concurrently matters:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import asyncio, json\n\nasync def run_calls(tool_calls):\n    async def one(call):\n        try:\n            result = await ASYNC_TOOLS[call.function.name](**json.loads(call.function.arguments))\n            content = json.dumps(result)\n        except Exception as e:\n            content = json.dumps({\"error\": f\"{type(e).__name__}: {e}\"})\n        return {\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call.id, \"content\": content}\n    return await asyncio.gather(*(one(c) for c in tool_calls))   # keeps the original order\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A worked latency example: three independent lookups of 400 ms each take 1.2 s sequentially and about 0.4 s concurrently. The bigger saving is in model turns: three calls in one turn need one extra model round trip, while three calls in three turns need three, each costing a second or more and resending the whole conversation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Anthropic suggests prompting for it when you want more parallelism, for example: \"whenever you need to perform multiple independent operations, invoke all relevant tools simultaneously rather than sequentially.\"\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Turn parallel calls off\u003c/b> when operations depend on each other or have side effects whose order matters (refund, then email the customer), when a backend is rate-limited, or when a small model tends to duplicate calls:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>OpenAI\u003c/th>\u003cth>Anthropic\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Disable parallel calls\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>\"parallel_tool_calls\": false\u003c/code> (zero or one call per turn)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>\"tool_choice\": {\"type\": \"auto\", \"disable_parallel_tool_use\": true}\u003c/code> (at most one call; exactly one with \u003ccode>any\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>tool\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Tool choice\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Behaviour\u003c/th>\u003cth>OpenAI Chat Completions\u003c/th>\u003cth>Anthropic\u003c/th>\u003cth>Gemini (\u003ccode>generateContent\u003c/code>)\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Model decides (default with tools)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>\"auto\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"type\": \"auto\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>mode \u003ccode>AUTO\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Must call at least one tool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>\"required\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"type\": \"any\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>mode \u003ccode>ANY\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Must call this tool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"search_docs\"}}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"search_docs\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>mode \u003ccode>ANY\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>allowedFunctionNames: [\"search_docs\"]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>No tool calls\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>\"none\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"type\": \"none\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>mode \u003ccode>NONE\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Restrict to a subset\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"type\": \"allowed_tools\", \"allowed_tools\": {\"mode\": \"auto\", \"tools\": [...]}}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Send fewer tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>allowedFunctionNames\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Gemini adds a \u003ccode>VALIDATED\u003c/code> mode: the model may answer in text or call a function, and any call it makes is schema-checked with constrained decoding. OpenAI's Responses API takes the same options in a flatter shape, for example \u003ccode>{\"type\": \"function\", \"name\": \"search_docs\"}\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to force, and the catches\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Good reasons to force a tool: the first step of a workflow must be a search; a router must pick exactly one action; you need a guaranteed call in a test. Forcing is a blunt instrument, though. Ask \"hi\" with \u003ccode>required\u003c/code> set and the model will call \u003ci>some\u003c/i> tool anyway.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Catches to know, as of late 2026:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>No text with a forced call on Anthropic.\u003c/b> With \u003ccode>any\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>tool\u003c/code>, the API prefills the assistant turn, so the model emits the tool call without any explanation first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Thinking conflicts.\u003c/b> Anthropic does not support \u003ccode>any\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>tool\u003c/code> with manual extended thinking, and its newest models (Claude Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5 and the Fable and Mythos tiers) reject forced tool use with a 400 error regardless of thinking. The documented replacement is \u003ccode>auto\u003c/code> with strict tools to guarantee valid inputs, or structured outputs when you need a fixed JSON shape.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Caching.\u003c/b> On Anthropic, changing \u003ccode>tool_choice\u003c/code> between requests invalidates cached message blocks. On OpenAI, \u003ccode>allowed_tools\u003c/code> exists precisely so you can restrict tools per turn \u003ci>without\u003c/i> changing the \u003ccode>tools\u003c/code> list and losing the cached prefix.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Extraction by forced tool is legacy.\u003c/b> Forcing a single \"record_invoice\" tool used to be the standard way to get JSON. Structured outputs now do this directly.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you force a specific tool, and how do you allow several tool calls in one turn?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>To force a particular tool, set \u003ccode>tool_choice\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"search_docs\"}}\u003c/code> on OpenAI Chat Completions or \u003ccode>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"search_docs\"}\u003c/code> on Anthropic (where the model supports forced tool use). To require at least one call while allowing several, use \u003ccode>\"required\"\u003c/code> on OpenAI or \u003ccode>{\"type\": \"any\"}\u003c/code> on Anthropic and leave parallel calls on (OpenAI \u003ccode>parallel_tool_calls\u003c/code> defaults to true; on Anthropic do not set \u003ccode>disable_parallel_tool_use\u003c/code>). Then execute all returned calls, concurrently where they are independent, and return every result before the next model call.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why might you disable parallel tool calls for a payments assistant?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Payment actions have side effects and ordering constraints (check the balance, then charge, then send a receipt). Running them in one batch can execute a step whose precondition failed, so you force one call per turn and let the model see each result before the next action.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A request to one of Anthropic's newest models with \u003ccode>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"tool\", ...}\u003c/code> returns a 400 error. What do you use instead?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"auto\"}\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>strict: true\u003c/code> on the tool so any call has a schema-valid input, steer the model towards the tool in the prompt, or switch to structured outputs if what you really need is a fixed JSON response.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:function-calling-across-providers":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every provider implements the same idea: a tool has a \u003cb>name\u003c/b>, a \u003cb>description\u003c/b> and a \u003cb>JSON Schema\u003c/b> for its parameters; the model returns a call with a name, arguments and an id; you return a result tied to that id. The differences are in the envelope: field names, where calls appear, whether arguments are a string or an object, and which role carries the result. Keep one neutral definition in your code (a Pydantic model) and convert at the edge.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The envelopes side by side\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>OpenAI Chat Completions\u003c/th>\u003cth>OpenAI Responses\u003c/th>\u003cth>Anthropic Messages\u003c/th>\u003cth>Gemini \u003ccode>generateContent\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tool definition\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {name, description, parameters}}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"type\": \"function\", name, description, parameters}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{name, description, input_schema}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"functionDeclarations\": [{name, description, parameters}]}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Where calls appear\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>message.tool_calls[]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>output[]\u003c/code> items of type \u003ccode>function_call\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>content[]\u003c/code> blocks of type \u003ccode>tool_use\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>parts[]\u003c/code> containing \u003ccode>functionCall\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Arguments\u003c/td>\u003ctd>JSON \u003cb>string\u003c/b> in \u003ccode>function.arguments\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>JSON \u003cb>string\u003c/b> in \u003ccode>arguments\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Object\u003c/b> in \u003ccode>input\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Object\u003c/b> in \u003ccode>args\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Call id\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>id\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>call_id\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>id\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>id\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Result goes in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\", \"content\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"type\": \"function_call_output\", \"call_id\", \"output\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>user message with \u003ccode>{\"type\": \"tool_result\", \"tool_use_id\", \"content\", \"is_error\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>a content entry with \u003ccode>functionResponse: {id, name, response}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"I am waiting for tools\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>finish_reason: \"tool_calls\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>function_call\u003c/code> items in output\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>stop_reason: \"tool_use\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>functionCall\u003c/code> parts present\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Strict schemas\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>\"strict\": true\u003c/code> per function\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>\"strict\": true\u003c/code> per function\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>\"strict\": true\u003c/code> per tool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>mode \u003ccode>VALIDATED\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Google's newer \u003cb>Interactions API\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>client.interactions.create\u003c/code>) uses yet another shape, close to OpenAI's Responses: tools are \u003ccode>{\"type\": \"function\", name, description, parameters}\u003c/code>, the model emits \u003ccode>function_call\u003c/code> steps with an \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>name\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>arguments\u003c/code>, and you reply with a \u003ccode>function_result\u003c/code> input carrying the matching \u003ccode>call_id\u003c/code>. Both Gemini interfaces are documented as of late 2026.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>One definition, four formats\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nclass GetOrderStatus(BaseModel):\n    \"\"\"Look up the shipping status of one order by its order number.\n    Returns status, carrier and estimated delivery date.\"\"\"\n    order_id: str = Field(description=\"Digits only, e.g. 8812\")\n\ndef neutral(name, model):\n    return {\"name\": name, \"description\": \" \".join(model.__doc__.split()),\n            \"schema\": model.model_json_schema()}\n\ndef for_openai_chat(t):\n    return {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n        \"name\": t[\"name\"], \"description\": t[\"description\"], \"parameters\": t[\"schema\"]}}\n\ndef for_openai_responses(t):\n    return {\"type\": \"function\", \"name\": t[\"name\"], \"description\": t[\"description\"],\n            \"parameters\": t[\"schema\"]}\n\ndef for_anthropic(t):\n    return {\"name\": t[\"name\"], \"description\": t[\"description\"], \"input_schema\": t[\"schema\"]}\n\ndef for_gemini(t):   # goes inside tools=[{\"functionDeclarations\": [...]}]\n    return {\"name\": t[\"name\"], \"description\": t[\"description\"],\n            \"parametersJsonSchema\": t[\"schema\"]}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>And the results, which differ more than the definitions:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def result_openai_chat(call_id, text):\n    return {\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call_id, \"content\": text}\n\ndef result_openai_responses(call_id, text):\n    return {\"type\": \"function_call_output\", \"call_id\": call_id, \"output\": text}\n\ndef result_anthropic(results):            # [(tool_use_id, text, is_error), ...], all in ONE message\n    return {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [\n        {\"type\": \"tool_result\", \"tool_use_id\": i, \"content\": t, \"is_error\": e} for i, t, e in results]}\n\ndef result_gemini(call_id, name, response: dict):\n    return {\"role\": \"user\", \"parts\": [{\"functionResponse\": {\"id\": call_id, \"name\": name, \"response\": response}}]}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Notice the details the converter has to get right: Anthropic wants every result for a turn in a single user message, with the results before any text; Gemini wants the response as an object, not a string; OpenAI wants one message per call.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Gotchas when porting\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>String versus object arguments.\u003c/b> OpenAI gives you a JSON string to parse; Anthropic and Gemini give you a parsed object. A port that forgets \u003ccode>json.loads\u003c/code> (or adds one too many) fails on the first call.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Schema dialects.\u003c/b> Gemini's classic \u003ccode>parameters\u003c/code> field accepts an OpenAPI-style subset of JSON Schema; \u003ccode>parametersJsonSchema\u003c/code> accepts JSON Schema directly. OpenAI's strict mode needs every property required and \u003ccode>additionalProperties: false\u003c/code>; Anthropic accepts optional properties. Generate from Pydantic and test each provider.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Name rules.\u003c/b> OpenAI allows letters, digits, underscores and dashes up to 64 characters; Anthropic allows the same characters up to 128. Stick to short snake_case names and they work everywhere.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ordering rules.\u003c/b> Anthropic rejects a conversation where tool results do not immediately follow the matching \u003ccode>tool_use\u003c/code> blocks. OpenAI rejects tool messages without a preceding call.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reasoning state.\u003c/b> Gemini's thinking models attach \u003ci>thought signatures\u003c/i> to their parts; when you build history by hand, send the model's parts back exactly as received (the SDKs do this for you). Reasoning models on other providers have similar rules about returning reasoning items or thinking blocks unchanged.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Error flags.\u003c/b> Anthropic has an explicit \u003ccode>is_error: true\u003c/code>; elsewhere you signal errors inside the result content, for example \u003ccode>{\"error\": \"...\"}\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Compatibility layers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You can often avoid hand-written converters. Anthropic and Gemini both offer OpenAI-compatible endpoints, and gateways such as LiteLLM translate one format into all the others (covered in the Working with model APIs module). The trade-off is the usual one: provider-specific features (strict tools, thinking blocks, server tools, cache controls) may be dropped or translated imperfectly. Teams commonly use a gateway for breadth and write a native adapter for the one or two providers they depend on most.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you port the same tool definitions to a second provider?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keep one neutral definition per tool (name, description, JSON Schema generated from a Pydantic model) and write small converters to each provider's envelope: \u003ccode>function.parameters\u003c/code> for OpenAI Chat Completions, \u003ccode>input_schema\u003c/code> for Anthropic, \u003ccode>functionDeclarations\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>parametersJsonSchema\u003c/code> for Gemini. Convert calls coming back (string versus object arguments, different id fields) and results going out (a \u003ccode>tool\u003c/code> message, a \u003ccode>tool_result\u003c/code> block in a user message, or a \u003ccode>functionResponse\u003c/code> part), then run the same tool-use test cases on both providers, because schema dialects and ordering rules differ.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your agent works on OpenAI but fails on Anthropic after the model makes two tool calls in one turn. What is a likely cause?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The two results were sent as two separate user messages, or text was placed before the \u003ccode>tool_result\u003c/code> blocks. Anthropic expects all results for a turn in a single user message, results first, each with the matching \u003ccode>tool_use_id\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why are tool-call arguments a string on OpenAI but an object on Anthropic, and why does it matter?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is an API design choice: OpenAI returns the raw generated JSON text, while Anthropic and Gemini return it already parsed. Your code must parse on one and not on the other, and on OpenAI a parse failure (without strict mode) is one more error path to handle.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:tools/built-in-server-side-tools":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Tools differ by \u003cb>where the code runs\u003c/b>. \u003cb>Client tools\u003c/b> run in your application: the model asks, you execute, you return the result. \u003cb>Server tools\u003c/b> run on the provider's infrastructure inside the same API call: you enable them with one line, and the model searches the web, runs code or searches files before it answers. Built-ins save you building and hosting common capabilities; in exchange you accept extra fees, less control over sources and data leaving through another party.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Client and server tools\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Client tool\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model asks for a call\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Your code runs it\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Result sent back\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model answers\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Server tool\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model asks for a call\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Provider runs it in the same request\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model answers with citations\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With a server tool there is no round trip through your code. The response contains a record of what happened (for example the searches made and the sources found) and the final answer, usually with citations.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What is built in (as of late 2026)\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Capability\u003c/th>\u003cth>OpenAI (Responses API)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Anthropic\u003c/th>\u003cth>Gemini\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Web search\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>web_search\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>web_search\u003c/code> (server tool)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Google Search grounding\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Read a given URL\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Not a separate tool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>web_fetch\u003c/code> (server tool)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>URL context\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Run code in a sandbox\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>code_interpreter\u003c/code>, hosted \u003ccode>shell\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>code_execution\u003c/code> (server tool)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Code execution\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Search your uploaded files\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>file_search\u003c/code> over vector stores\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Not built in; use your own retrieval\u003c/td>\u003ctd>File Search\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Remote MCP servers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>mcp\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>MCP connector\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>mcp_server\u003c/code> (Interactions API)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Operate a computer or browser\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>computer\u003c/code> (you execute the actions)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Computer and browser toolsets (client)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Computer Use (client side)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Load tools on demand\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tool_search\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tool_search_tool_regex\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>_bm25\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Not built in\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Anthropic's server tools are versioned by date in their \u003ccode>type\u003c/code> field (for example \u003ccode>web_search_20250305\u003c/code>, with newer versions adding features), so you pin the version you tested. Anthropic also publishes \u003ci>Anthropic-schema client tools\u003c/i>, such as \u003ccode>bash\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>text_editor\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>memory\u003c/code>: the model was trained on their exact schema, but your code executes them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Turning one on\u003c/h4>\u003cp>OpenAI Responses API, Python:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\nr = client.responses.create(model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", tools=[{\"type\": \"web_search\"}],\n                            input=\"What changed in the latest Kubernetes minor release?\")\nprint(r.output_text)    # output also contains web_search_call items and url_citation annotations\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Anthropic Messages API, the tool entry:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\"type\": \"web_search_20250305\", \"name\": \"web_search\", \"max_uses\": 5,\n \"allowed_domains\": [\"kubernetes.io\", \"github.com\"]}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>max_uses\u003c/code> caps the number of searches per request, and \u003ccode>allowed_domains\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>blocked_domains\u003c/code>, not both) restricts sources. The response contains \u003ccode>server_tool_use\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>web_search_tool_result\u003c/code> blocks, then the answer with citations.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The server-side loop\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A server tool can take several steps inside one request: search, read, search again, then answer. On Anthropic, a long-running turn may stop with \u003ccode>stop_reason: \"pause_turn\"\u003c/code>. That is not an error: send the conversation back (including the paused assistant content) and the server continues where it left off. Your loop must treat \u003ccode>pause_turn\u003c/code> as \"call again\", not as a final answer.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Mixed turns also exist: the model can call a server tool and one of your client tools in the same turn, in which case you execute your part and return its result as usual.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Built-in or your own?\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Built-in server tool\u003c/th>\u003cth>Your own client tool\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Nothing to build, host or scale\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Full control over sources, ranking and freshness\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Provider-tuned prompts and result formats, usually with citations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Works the same across providers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Lower latency: no round trip through your app\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your data stays in your systems\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Extra usage fees on top of tokens (for example per search or for container time)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You pay for your own infrastructure instead\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Data goes to the provider and possibly a search partner; check data-retention terms per tool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Easier to log, evaluate and debug every step\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A sensible default: use built-ins for \u003cb>generic\u003c/b> capabilities (public web search, a throwaway Python sandbox for calculations or charts) and your own tools for \u003cb>your\u003c/b> systems and data (your docs, tickets, orders). Atlas follows that rule.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Computer use in one paragraph\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Computer-use tools let the model operate a graphical interface: it receives a screenshot, returns an action (\"click at 412, 230\", \"type hello\"), your harness performs it in a sandboxed virtual machine or browser, and you send back the next screenshot. It is slow and token-hungry (every step includes an image), and every web page it reads can carry a prompt injection, so run it isolated and require human approval for consequential actions. The Agents module covers it in depth.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between a client tool and a server tool?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A client tool runs in your application: the model returns a call, your code executes it and sends the result back in the next request. A server tool runs on the provider's infrastructure within the same request, so the response already contains the tool's results and the final answer, with no execution step in your code.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your Anthropic request with web search returns \u003ccode>stop_reason: \"pause_turn\"\u003c/code>. What do you do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Treat it as an unfinished turn: send a new request with the conversation including the paused assistant content, and the server-side loop continues. Do not show the partial response as the final answer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you build your own search tool instead of using the built-in web search?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the answers must come from your own or restricted sources, when data must not pass through a third party, when you need the same behaviour across providers, or when you need full control over ranking, freshness, logging and evaluation of the search step.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:advanced-tool-use-tool-search-and-examples":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every tool definition you send is read by the model on every call, and every tool result stays in the conversation. With dozens of tools or bulky results, that overhead can dwarf the actual task. \u003cb>Tool search\u003c/b> keeps most definitions out of the context until the model searches for them (\u003ci>deferred loading\u003c/i>). \u003cb>Programmatic tool calling\u003c/b> lets the model write code that calls tools inside a sandbox, so intermediate results never enter its context. \u003cb>Tool use examples\u003c/b> show the model how to fill complex parameters.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The problem in numbers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Anthropic's example: five common MCP servers (GitHub with 35 tools, Slack with 11, Sentry, Grafana and Splunk) add up to 58 tools and roughly \u003cb>55,000 tokens of definitions\u003c/b> before the conversation starts. Jira alone adds about 17,000. Anthropic has seen tool definitions reach 134,000 tokens in its own setups before optimisation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Do the arithmetic for an agent. Fifty tools at about 1,000 tokens each is 50,000 tokens per call; an agent run of 20 model calls sends 1,000,000 tokens of tool definitions. Prompt caching cuts the price (the Billing module), but not the context crowding: the model must pick the right tool from 50 similar ones on every step, and accuracy suffers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Tool search: load definitions on demand\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Request\u003c/b>\u003csmall>search tool plus 50 deferred tools\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model sees only the search tool and a few core tools\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model searches\u003c/b>\u003csmall>'create jira issue'\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Matching definitions are loaded\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model calls the tool\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>On Anthropic, you add a tool search tool and mark the rest \u003ccode>defer_loading: true\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\"tools\": [\n  {\"type\": \"tool_search_tool_regex_20251119\", \"name\": \"tool_search_tool_regex\"},\n  {\"name\": \"jira_create_issue\", \"description\": \"Create a Jira issue in a given project ...\",\n   \"input_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"...\": \"...\"}}, \"defer_loading\": true},\n  {\"name\": \"github_list_pull_requests\", \"description\": \"List open pull requests ...\",\n   \"input_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"...\": \"...\"}}, \"defer_loading\": true}\n]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>You still send every definition in every request, because the API needs them to run the search, but deferred tools are not placed in the model's context. When the model searches (by regex, or with the BM25 variant by natural-language query), the API returns up to five matches by default as \u003ccode>tool_reference\u003c/code> blocks and expands them into full definitions. Anthropic recommends keeping your three to five most-used tools non-deferred so common calls need no search. Because deferred tools are excluded from the prompt prefix, adding or discovering them does not break prompt caching.\u003c/p>\u003cp>OpenAI's version (Responses API, recent models) adds \u003ccode>{\"type\": \"tool_search\"}\u003c/code> to the tools array and marks functions \u003ccode>defer_loading: true\u003c/code>. It recommends grouping deferred functions into \u003cb>namespaces\u003c/b> (or MCP servers) with clear descriptions, ideally fewer than ten functions each: at the start the model sees only namespace names and descriptions, then loads the functions it needs. Search can be hosted by OpenAI or executed by your own code when the available tools depend on tenant or project state.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The reported effect: in Anthropic's example, context used by tool definitions fell from about 77,000 tokens to about 8,700, an 85% reduction, and accuracy on its MCP evaluations rose from 49% to 74% for Claude Opus 4 and from 79.5% to 88.1% for Claude Opus 4.5. Tool search is worth it when you have more than about 10 to 20 tools or more than about 10,000 tokens of definitions; with five tools, the extra search step only adds latency.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Programmatic tool calling: keep intermediate results out\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Classic tool calling sends every result through the model. To check which of 20 employees exceeded their travel budget, the model would call the expense tool 20 times and read thousands of line items. With \u003cb>programmatic tool calling (PTC)\u003c/b>, the model writes a program instead:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode># A sketch of code the model writes. It runs in the provider's sandbox, where each\n# allowed tool is an async function that returns the tool result as text.\nimport asyncio, json\nteam = json.loads(await get_team_members({\"department\": \"engineering\"}))\nresults = await asyncio.gather(*[get_expenses({\"user_id\": m[\"id\"], \"quarter\": \"Q3\"}) for m in team])\nover = [m[\"name\"] for m, r in zip(team, results)\n        if sum(item[\"amount\"] for item in json.loads(r)) &gt; m[\"budget\"]]\nprint(over)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each tool call from the program pauses execution and comes to \u003ci>your\u003c/i> code like a normal tool call (marked with a \u003ccode>caller\u003c/code> field), you return the result, and the program continues. The thousands of line items stay inside the sandbox; only the final \u003ccode>print\u003c/code> reaches the model. Anthropic's example cut 200 KB of raw expense data to 1 KB of results, and on complex research tasks average usage dropped from 43,588 to 27,297 tokens (37%) while accuracy improved (for example from 25.6% to 28.5% on an internal knowledge-retrieval benchmark).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Provider\u003c/th>\u003cth>How you enable it\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Anthropic\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Add the code execution tool (for example \u003ccode>{\"type\": \"code_execution_20260120\", \"name\": \"code_execution\"}\u003c/code>) and set \u003ccode>\"allowed_callers\": [\"code_execution_20260120\"]\u003c/code> on tools the program may call\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Add the \u003ccode>programmatic_tool_calling\u003c/code> hosted tool and set \u003ccode>allowed_callers\u003c/code> on eligible tools; programs run as JavaScript in an isolated V8 runtime\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>OpenAI's guide gives a clear rule for choosing: use PTC when code can filter, join, aggregate or validate several results into a smaller output and the control flow is predictable; use direct calls for a single lookup, when each result needs fresh model judgement, for writes and approval-sensitive actions, and when citations must be preserved. Check data-retention terms as well: at the time of writing Anthropic lists PTC as not eligible for zero data retention, while OpenAI documents ZDR support for it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Tool use examples\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Some parameters are hard to describe: nested objects, date formats, optional fields that go together. Anthropic's \u003ccode>input_examples\u003c/code> field lets you attach a few schema-valid example inputs to a tool definition. In its internal testing, examples raised accuracy on complex parameter handling from 72% to 90%. Use them for complex tools, after the description is already good.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Explain how deferred tool loading saves context.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Normally every tool definition is placed in the model's context on every call, so 50 tools can cost tens of thousands of tokens per call before any work starts. With deferred loading, tools marked \u003ccode>defer_loading: true\u003c/code> are kept out of the context and the model sees only a search tool (plus a few core tools or namespace descriptions). When it needs a capability it searches, and only the handful of matching definitions are loaded. In Anthropic's example this cut tool-definition context by about 85% and improved tool-selection accuracy, and because deferred tools are not part of the cached prefix, discovering them does not invalidate the prompt cache.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When is programmatic tool calling the wrong choice?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For a single simple lookup, when each result needs the model's judgement before deciding the next step, for writes or actions that need approval, and when native citations or artefacts must reach the model intact. In those cases direct tool calls keep a clear decision and authorisation boundary.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">An agent has 40 tools averaging 800 tokens each and makes 25 model calls per task. How many definition tokens does it send without tool search, and roughly how many with it if each call needs about 3 tools loaded?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Without tool search: 40 × 800 = 32,000 tokens per call, × 25 = 800,000 tokens per task. With tool search: about 3 × 800 = 2,400 tokens of loaded definitions plus a small search tool per call, × 25 = roughly 60,000 to 70,000 tokens, more than a tenfold reduction.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:tools/the-tool-loop-by-hand":"\u003cp>No framework and no SDK: you will send the JSON yourself and print what comes back, so every message in the loop is visible. The code works with any OpenAI-compatible endpoint, including OpenAI, OpenRouter, vLLM and Ollama.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.11+ and \u003ccode>pip install httpx\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>Environment variables: \u003ccode>BASE_URL\u003c/code> (for example \u003ccode>https://api.openai.com/v1\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>http://localhost:11434/v1\u003c/code> for Ollama), \u003ccode>API_KEY\u003c/code> (any string for Ollama) and \u003ccode>MODEL\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a local model, pick one whose model page lists tool support; small models are noticeably weaker at tool calling\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>loop.py\u003c/code>. It has two tools, an order lookup and a currency converter, with fake data so results are predictable.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json, os, sys\nimport httpx\n\nBASE_URL, API_KEY, MODEL = os.environ[\"BASE_URL\"], os.environ[\"API_KEY\"], os.environ[\"MODEL\"]\n\nORDERS = {\"8812\": {\"currency\": \"GBP\", \"items\": [{\"name\": \"Desk lamp\", \"price\": 34.0},\n                                                {\"name\": \"Bulbs\", \"price\": 6.5}]},\n          \"9031\": {\"currency\": \"USD\", \"items\": [{\"name\": \"Keyboard\", \"price\": 89.0}]}}\nRATES = {(\"GBP\", \"EUR\"): 1.16, (\"USD\", \"EUR\"): 0.92}          # fixed rates for the lab\n\ndef get_order(order_id):\n    if order_id not in ORDERS:\n        return {\"error\": f\"No order {order_id}. Order numbers have four digits, for example 8812.\"}\n    o = ORDERS[order_id]\n    return {\"order_id\": order_id, \"currency\": o[\"currency\"], \"items\": o[\"items\"],\n            \"total\": sum(i[\"price\"] for i in o[\"items\"])}\n\ndef convert_currency(amount, from_currency, to_currency):\n    rate = RATES.get((from_currency, to_currency))\n    if rate is None:\n        return {\"error\": f\"No rate for {from_currency} to {to_currency}. Supported: GBP to EUR, USD to EUR.\"}\n    return {\"amount\": round(amount * rate, 2), \"currency\": to_currency}\n\nTOOLS = {\"get_order\": get_order, \"convert_currency\": convert_currency}\nSPECS = [\n    {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n        \"name\": \"get_order\",\n        \"description\": \"Get one order by its four-digit order number: items, prices, currency and total.\",\n        \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                       \"required\": [\"order_id\"], \"additionalProperties\": False}}},\n    {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n        \"name\": \"convert_currency\",\n        \"description\": \"Convert an amount between currencies at today's rate. Supports GBP to EUR and USD to EUR.\",\n        \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\n            \"amount\": {\"type\": \"number\"},\n            \"from_currency\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"GBP\", \"USD\"]},\n            \"to_currency\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"EUR\"]}},\n            \"required\": [\"amount\", \"from_currency\", \"to_currency\"], \"additionalProperties\": False}}},\n]\n\ndef chat(messages, **extra):\n    r = httpx.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/chat/completions\", timeout=60,\n                   headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {API_KEY}\"},\n                   json={\"model\": MODEL, \"messages\": messages, \"tools\": SPECS, **extra})\n    r.raise_for_status()\n    return r.json()\n\ndef run(question, max_steps=6, **extra):\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a shop assistant. Use the tools; never guess prices.\"},\n                {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]\n    for step in range(1, max_steps + 1):\n        data = chat(messages, **extra)\n        choice, usage = data[\"choices\"][0], data.get(\"usage\", {})\n        msg = choice[\"message\"]\n        print(f\"--- step {step}: finish_reason={choice['finish_reason']}, \"\n              f\"prompt_tokens={usage.get('prompt_tokens')}\")\n        calls = msg.get(\"tool_calls\") or []\n        if not calls:\n            print(\"ANSWER:\", msg.get(\"content\"))\n            return messages\n        messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": msg.get(\"content\"), \"tool_calls\": calls})\n        for c in calls:\n            args = json.loads(c[\"function\"][\"arguments\"])\n            result = TOOLS[c[\"function\"][\"name\"]](**args)\n            print(\"  CALL\", c[\"function\"][\"name\"], args, \"-&gt;\", result)\n            messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": c[\"id\"], \"content\": json.dumps(result)})\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"no answer after {max_steps} steps\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    transcript = run(sys.argv[1])\n    print(json.dumps(transcript, indent=2))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Run a question that needs two tools in sequence and read the printed transcript from top to bottom:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>python loop.py \"What did order 8812 cost in euros?\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Run a question with independent sub-tasks: \u003ccode>python loop.py \"What did orders 8812 and 9031 cost in euros altogether?\"\u003c/code>. Look for a step with two \u003ccode>CALL\u003c/code> lines: that is a parallel tool call. Find the two \u003ccode>tool\u003c/code> messages and match their \u003ccode>tool_call_id\u003c/code> values to the call ids.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run an error path: \u003ccode>python loop.py \"What did order 1234 cost?\"\u003c/code>. Read how the model uses the error text.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Turn parallel calls off and repeat step 3. In the \u003ccode>__main__\u003c/code> block, call \u003ccode>run(sys.argv[1], parallel_tool_calls=False)\u003c/code>. Count the steps.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Force tool use on a greeting: call \u003ccode>run(\"Hi there!\", tool_choice=\"required\")\u003c/code>. Note what the model calls and why that is a bad default.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Hit the budget: call \u003ccode>run(\"What did orders 8812 and 9031 cost in euros altogether?\", max_steps=1)\u003c/code> and see the loop stop with an error.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Make one more change and observe it: replace the helpful error in \u003ccode>get_order\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>{\"error\": \"not found\"}\u003c/code> and rerun step 4.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Look at \u003ccode>prompt_tokens\u003c/code> across the steps of any run and write down how it grows.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Step 2 takes three model calls: \u003ccode>get_order\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>convert_currency\u003c/code> with the total from the first result, then the answer (about €46.98 for £40.50 at 1.16).\u003c/li>\u003cli>On capable models, step 3 shows two \u003ccode>get_order\u003c/code> calls in one step, then two conversions in the next, then the answer. Smaller local models may make the calls one at a time, skip a tool or produce invalid arguments.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With the helpful error, the model tells the user the order was not found and asks for a four-digit number; with the bare \"not found\" it usually still apologises but gives less useful guidance.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With parallel calls off, step 3 takes about five model calls instead of three.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>tool_choice=\"required\"\u003c/code> on \"Hi there!\" produces a pointless \u003ccode>get_order\u003c/code> call with an invented or empty order number.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>prompt_tokens\u003c/code> grows every step, because each request resends the whole conversation plus both tool definitions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does \u003ccode>prompt_tokens\u003c/code> grow with every step of the loop?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The API is stateless: each request carries the system prompt, the tool definitions, the user message and every earlier tool call and result. Each step adds an assistant message and one or more tool results, so the input grows; prompt caching makes the repeated prefix cheaper but not smaller.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In step 3, how did your code know which result belonged to which call?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each tool call has a unique id, and each \u003ccode>tool\u003c/code> message carries that id in \u003ccode>tool_call_id\u003c/code>. The model matches results to calls by id, not by order or by tool name.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:document-extraction-service":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Starting point: Atlas after M3. You have a provider-agnostic client with structured logs (M1), a cost and cache meter (M2), and versioned prompts with a regression suite running in CI (M3). Atlas can answer from documents you paste in, but it cannot look anything up or take an action.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This milestone adds two capabilities. \u003cb>Typed tools:\u003c/b> Atlas can search its document collection, read a document, look up a record and, after the user confirms, create a ticket, through a tool loop you wrote and control. \u003cb>Structured extraction:\u003c/b> send Atlas a document (an invoice, a receipt or a support email) and get back validated, typed JSON, backed by an accuracy report on 50 labelled documents.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Tools:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>atlas/tools/\u003c/code> with one Pydantic argument model per tool, and tool specs generated from those models for at least two formats (OpenAI-style and Anthropic-style).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Four tools:\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>search_docs(query, max_results)\u003c/code>: keyword search over your corpus (BM25 with \u003ccode>rank-bm25\u003c/code>, or SQLite FTS5), returning ids, titles and short snippets. Hybrid search replaces it in M7.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>get_doc(doc_id, section)\u003c/code>: returns a bounded amount of text with a truncation note.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>lookup_record(kind, record_id)\u003c/code>: read-only lookup in a SQLite or JSON fixture of tickets or users.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>create_ticket(title, body, priority)\u003c/code>: a write action that runs only after the user confirms.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>atlas/tool_loop.py\u003c/code>: the loop with a step limit, concurrent execution of read-only calls, argument validation, a permission check for write tools, errors returned as results, and a per-run cost budget using the M2 meter (for example, stop above $0.05).\u003c/li>\u003cli>A log line per tool call: tool name, validated arguments (with sensitive fields redacted), latency, result size in tokens and an error flag.\u003c/li>\u003cli>At least 10 tool-use cases added to the M3 suite: the right tool is chosen, arguments are valid, no write happens without confirmation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Extraction:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>POST /extract\u003c/code> (FastAPI) accepting \u003ccode>{\"schema\": \"invoice\" | \"support_email\", \"text\": \"...\"}\u003c/code> and returning either \u003ccode>{\"status\": \"ok\", \"data\": {...}}\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>{\"status\": \"needs_review\", \"errors\": [...]}\u003c/code>. It never returns unvalidated output.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Pydantic models with validators for business rules (totals add up, dates are plausible).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Strict structured outputs where the provider supports them; JSON mode plus validation and up to two re-asks for a local model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>50 labelled documents in \u003ccode>evals/extraction/\u003c/code> as JSONL with expected fields: a public receipts dataset such as CORD or SROIE, synthetic invoices you generate and label, or your team's support emails with personal data removed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>python -m atlas.eval_extraction\u003c/code> prints, for at least two models: accuracy per field, whole-document exact match, first-pass validity, retry rate, \u003ccode>needs_review\u003c/code> rate, cost per document and p50 and p95 latency.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Write the argument models and spec generation for two formats, with unit tests on the generated specs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implement the four tools with compact, bounded results and helpful error messages.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build the tool loop with validation, permissions, concurrent reads, the budget and logging. Ask five realistic questions and read every transcript.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add the tool-use cases to the regression suite and get CI green.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build the extraction models, the endpoint and the repair ladder (local repair, re-ask, \u003ccode>needs_review\u003c/code>).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Label the 50 documents, run the report for two models and write up which fields fail, why, and what you changed to fix them.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Asking Atlas \"find the runbook for rotating the Kafka certificates and open a ticket to do it next Tuesday\" produces a search, a document read and a proposed ticket, and the ticket is created only after you confirm; the log shows each call with its cost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A tool error, such as an unknown record id, is returned to the model and handled sensibly in the answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>POST /extract\u003c/code> returns validated data or \u003ccode>needs_review\u003c/code>, never raw model output.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One command produces the extraction report with field accuracy, validity, retry and cost figures for two models.\u003c/li>\u003cli>CI passes, including the new tool-use cases.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Implement the loop natively for a second provider (Anthropic's \u003ccode>tool_use\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>tool_result\u003c/code> blocks) and run the same tool-use cases on both.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add tool use examples or few-shot examples for the hardest tool and measure the change in argument validity.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Give \u003ccode>get_doc\u003c/code> a \u003ccode>response_format\u003c/code> of \u003ccode>concise\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>detailed\u003c/code> and measure the tokens saved.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add an idempotency key to \u003ccode>create_ticket\u003c/code> and prove that a retried call does not create a duplicate.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Validate and authorise inside one \u003ccode>execute\u003c/code> function, so no code path can skip either step:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from pydantic import ValidationError\n\nWRITE_TOOLS = {\"create_ticket\"}\n\ndef execute(name, raw_args, ctx):\n    if name not in ARG_MODELS:\n        return {\"error\": f\"Unknown tool {name}. Available tools: {', '.join(ARG_MODELS)}.\"}\n    try:\n        args = ARG_MODELS[name].model_validate_json(raw_args)   # model_validate(dict) on Anthropic\n    except ValidationError as e:\n        return {\"error\": \"Invalid arguments:\\n\" + format_errors(e)}\n    if name in WRITE_TOOLS and not ctx.is_confirmed(name, args):\n        return {\"error\": \"Not executed: the user has not confirmed. Show the proposed ticket \"\n                         \"and ask the user to confirm it.\"}\n    return IMPLS[name](args, user=ctx.user)        # the user comes from the session, never from the model\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For field-level accuracy, normalise before comparing: trim and lowercase strings, convert dates to ISO format and round amounts to cents. Otherwise \"ACME Ltd\" against \"Acme Ltd.\" counts as an error and hides the failures that matter.\u003c/p>"}]);
